2025-07-31T00-53-28-095Z

[{"xmlns":"nwws-oi","id":"nwws_processor.1553","issue":"2025-07-31T00:53:00Z","ttaaii":"FXUS61","cccc":"KRLX","awipsid":"AFDRLX"}]
=================================================



336

FXUS61 KRLX 310053

AFDRLX



AREA FORECAST DISCUSSION

National Weather Service Charleston WV

853 PM EDT Wed Jul 30 2025



.SYNOPSIS...

Persistent hot/humid conditions will continue into Thursday, with

a crossing cold front providing much cooler conditions for Friday.

Isolated/scattered afternoon/evening storms are possible until then.



&&



.NEAR TERM /THROUGH THURSDAY/...

As of 820 PM Wednesday...



The Extreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory were allowed to

expire at 8 PM. Otherwise, ISO showers/storms continue across 

the area, with some activity having developed further north. Any

ongoing showers/storms will begin to gradually diminish 

following sunset, with areas of valley fog (dense in spots) 

developing again tonight.



As of 520 PM Wednesday...



Scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms have initiated

throughout the afternoon along the spine of the higher terrain

via lift from anabatic convergence amid a moderately buoyant,

but very weakly forced environment, with very light low/mid 

level flow (KRLX VAD Wind Profile currently sampling less than 

10kts through 700mb) amid a subtle cap. Activity across the 

mountains continues to drift slowly northward along the 

associated cold pool. Expect this trend to continue to an 

extent, eventually slowing as associated (weak) gust fronts 

outrun shower/storm activity resulting in a general weakening 

trend. Locally gusty winds / heavy rain are possible through 

this evening, with a highly isolated water issue or two not out 

of the question. Given a few meandering outflow boundaries 

drifting northwards, can't entirely rule out a few isolated 

showers/storms further north this evening, but given the 

aforementioned cap, the probability appears to be on the lower 

end. Otherwise, a hot and humid evening continues, with heat 

indices currently in the mid 90s to low 100s across much of the 

lowlands.



As of 140 PM Wednesday...



Key Points:



 * Extreme Heat Warning and Heat Advisory remains in effect for

   the lowlands until 8 PM this evening.



 * Cold front brings a relief in heat conditions, with drier/cooler 

   airmass filtering in through the weekend. 



General guidance suggests the probability of heat index exceeding 

100 degrees this afternoon is high, around 70-80 percent. 

Therefore, heat headlines will continue until 8 PM this evening.



Isolated showers and thunderstorms are expected to develop once 

again this afternoon and evening across the area. This convection is 

triggered by ample humidity, heat and bouyancy under poor deep 

layered shear. These ingredients suggest slow-moving storm could 

produce localized heavy downpours this afternoon, but again, 

coverage should be limited. 



It will be muggy tonight with dewpoints in the low to mid 70s. The 

NBM shows a good signal for dense river valley fog development once 

again tonight. 



In terms of heat, abundant cloudiness and cooling showers should 

help to mitigate afternoon temperatures on Thursday, allowing heat 

index values to remain at or under the 100 degree mark. Therefore, 

decrease dewpoints Thursday afternoon as they look to high mid to 

upper 70s in order to lower heat index values. Since advisory 

criteria will not be met widespread, perhaps the event can be 

treated with a Special Weather Statement. Will let next shifts 

to decide whether an advisory may be needed for portions of the 

lowlands for Thursday. 



A cold front approaches from the northwest overnight, stalling 

across our north by Thursday morning. This feature will bring good 

chances for showers and storms on Thursday and Thursday night. Some 

storms could be strong to severe. SPC has portions of our northeast 

sections under a Marginal Risk for severe storms, while the rest of 

the area in under a general thunderstorms level.



&&



.SHORT TERM /THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...

As of 140 PM Wednesday...



By Friday, the front pushes further south away from the area, while 

a high pressure builds behind it. This high will bring cooler and 

drier airmass to the area through the end of the week, with near 

seasonal temperatures.



&&



.LONG TERM /FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...

As of 140 PM Wednesday...



Dry and pleasant weather is expected during the beginning of 

next week as a broad high pressure over the Great Lakes takes 

control. Then, general guidance bring a weak surface low 

pressure system and associated occluded front crossing our area 

from west to east Tuesday to Wednesday. Therefore, expect 

showers and storms to return by mid week with temperatures 

returning to near-seasonal normals.



&&



.AVIATION /00Z THURSDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...

As of 840 PM Wednesday...



ISO showers/storms continue across the area at present. Activity

will begin to gradually diminish over the next couple hours, 

giving way to a dry overnight thereafter. Brief MVFR/IFR VSBY 

and breezy winds are possible with any heavier showers/storms

this evening. The bigger concern for the overnight will be 

developing fog and perhaps some low stratus in/near the higher

terrain, with LIFR/VLIFR currently progged for all terminals.



Fog and low stratus will gradually lift/dissipate from ~ 11-14Z

on Thursday, bringing a return of mainly VFR outside of the 

potential for brief MVFR stratocu through late morning or early 

afternoon, and then the potential for MVFR CIGs across 

southeast Ohio and northern WV late in the TAF period. SCT 

showers/storms are possible during the afternoon/evening as a 

cold front begins pushing into the area, with the best chance 

for rain being central/northern locations. Given timing/location

uncertainty at this point, have just coded VCTS into most of 

the TAFs for the time being. MVFR/IFR VSBY is likely with any 

heavier showers or storms.



Surface flow goes calm tonight, with light and variable flow 

developing on Thursday, becoming primarily northwesterly by the

end of the TAF period.



FORECAST CONFIDENCE AND ALTERNATE SCENARIOS THROUGH 00Z FRIDAY...

     

FORECAST CONFIDENCE: Medium.

     

ALTERNATE SCENARIOS: Timing, extent, and density of fog overnight

may vary from the forecast. Brief MVFR/IFR VSBY is possible 

with showers/storms this evening and then again Thursday 

afternoon and evening. Return to VFR on Thursday could vary from

the forecast depending on morning / early afternoon stratocu.



